Z||000008||Eric||01-23-2001||12:08 AM||||Has anyone ever noticed any real difference in upgrading tire sizes like that.  I know they look cool and everything, but do the actually make the car handle better.  I know Travis said that smaller diameter wheels are better for acceleration, and his best attempts at cornering were on stock wheels with skinny, but sticky, yokohama tires.  Just wondering why everyone is so eager to part with $1000 or more.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||01-23-2001||02:07 AM||||16x7.5 with 225/50-16 front and 255/50-16 rear. eric, you gotta shop around. i have two similar sets of wheels on 2 fieros (antera 109s and panther 106s). i might have $1000 in both together, tires included.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FST2M6||01-23-2001||09:26 AM||||astetics! the Best later acceleration numbers i've ever had on my 86 SE were on 195-60-14s... the 17s are KILLER for reducing wheels spin and for getting a decent launch but the 14s are way better for everyday driving, cornering where the 17s are great only if your running lots of camber.. theres a saying "point of deminishing return" a 205-40 series tire will scrape the wheel before the side wall flexes. a 215-45 series will flex a bit more and so on. the rears on my car 235-40 deflect just as much at the 215-45s on the fron due to the side wall height. On DTM racing and the Cup lite and SCCA pro IT classes taht run 18-19" wheels they have 255-40 that have a taller side wall then I do which insreace their slip angle making the car more predictable and better in the turns.. my ture and wheels set up is for the relativly light cornering effort i put to it.. you can't race around a real corner on the street.. and this being a street car. i opted for pretty and shiney||||reg||1
